<script>
const KG = {"nodes":[{"id":"W1","type":"workflow","title":"W1 · Harness capability program","summary":"Archive-first search over harness variants: every run a stamped session, parents sampled from an archive that keeps losers, the mutator never touches the evaluator, promotion only through a staged paired experiment and an attributable human.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"W2","type":"workflow","title":"W2 · Certificate-first software factory","summary":"Compile the signed spec into hidden standards before anything else, run every goal as its own session under a program ledger, validate against a tree hash, release only what a certificate on the merged head proves.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"W3","type":"workflow","title":"W3 · Environment-first model pipeline","summary":"The unit of data is a group of rollouts of one environment under one policy version; rewards come from hidden checks on a pristine fixture plus the patch; every artefact down to the model card is generated from the same records.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"W4","type":"workflow","title":"W4 · Fleet observatory and two-speed improvement","summary":"A fast loop where agents refine skills in their department layer under a deterministic keep-or-retire scoreboard, and a slow loop where frozen experiments and attributable humans move knowledge into the fleet; the observatory is a projection of the log.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"P1","type":"principle","title":"P1 · Everything is a plugin","summary":"Knowledge, agents, tools, policies, judges, scoreboards, environments, and datasets are Cordis plugins; a workflow is a composition with lineage.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"P2","type":"principle","title":"P2 · Deterministic spine, semantic joints","summary":"Semantic stages never hold authority alone; a deterministic gate reads a durable event the implementer cannot forge; every semantic-to-deterministic hand-off is a validated structured output.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"P3","type":"principle","title":"P3 · The log is the dataset","summary":"Every metric, trajectory, and scoreboard row is recomputable from the session log; nothing model-visible bypasses it.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"P4","type":"principle","title":"P4 · The read barrier is authority, not a promise","summary":"A validator-owned root the implementer's executor cannot read, tool authority guards, and a certificate invariant replace an asserted 'the implementer never sees it'.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"P5","type":"principle","title":"P5 · Lineage and archive, content-addressed","summary":"Every component carries provenance and lineage; mutations mint new ids; the archive keeps every evaluated variant, losers included.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"P6","type":"principle","title":"P6 · Held-out discipline by content hash","summary":"Held-out environments never train and never reach improvement agents; decontamination keys on the stamp's content hashes.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"P7","type":"principle","title":"P7 · Budgets are explicit and durably enforced","summary":"Agent caps, token, wall-time, and cost budgets come from config; breaches block the goal durably and the observatory reads spend from the same logs.","source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:environments","type":"package","title":"dsh-environments","summary":"Registry of tasks with executable checks, held-out flags, provenance, an immutable set of check-owned paths the implementer must not author, and the environment/run stamp vocabulary.","workflows":["W1","W3"],"principles":["P1","P6"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:environment-runner","type":"package","title":"dsh-environment-runner","summary":"One environment as one fresh stamped session; the runner authors the standard, executes the checks, restores the fixture, hashes the tree, digests check-owned paths for tamper, clusters failures into a directive, certifies or directs.","workflows":["W1","W2","W3"],"principles":["P2","P3"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:verification","type":"package","title":"dsh-verification","summary":"Completion standards with optional weighted cases compared over four channels (exit, stdout, stderr, tree) through a closed set of pure normalizers; verification/run events carry parity, a passed/failed/tampered verdict, and the run's executor; certificates commit only after a fully passing run, capped at isolation none for an agent-reported one, plus clustered directives and relaxations.","workflows":["W1","W2","W3"],"principles":["P2","P3"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:fleet","type":"package","title":"dsh-fleet","summary":"Environment × model × repetition cells through the runner, with an optional exact cells selection so a resumed plan runs only its pending subset in place; error rows instead of missing rows; the observe-only fleet/cell event fires once per cell after its outcome and retention settle; a leaderboard per route and environment that never averages across isolation or the held-out split.","workflows":["W1"],"principles":["P2","P6"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:scorekeeper","type":"package","title":"dsh-scorekeeper","summary":"SessionFacts projection with a named source event per field, including district from the stamp and costEur/pricedSteps/pricingDigests folded from usage/priced alone; a leaderboard partitioned by route, environment, isolation, held-out split, and district with costEurPerCertified and a parity column kept separate from the certificate rate; pass@k per stamped batch; JSONL facts export.","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P3"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:experiments","type":"package","title":"dsh-experiments","summary":"Frozen paired plans over fleet cells, seeded bootstrap intervals on the certificate-rate delta, promote/reject/inconclusive verdicts, token budget refused before any cell runs.","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P2","P7"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:budget-policy","type":"package","title":"dsh-budget-policy","summary":"Token, wall-time, and cost caps folded from the log before each step, tightened by a session's own budget/caps record so an orchestrator can narrow but never widen what it configured; a budget/breach event and a durable goal block no round driver resumes; every priced step records a usage/priced event carrying its rates and pricing-table digest.","workflows":["W1","W2","W4"],"principles":["P7"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:read-barrier","type":"package","title":"dsh-read-barrier","summary":"Validator-owned root, per-session reservations, fs/read-intent denial at the executor, read-barrier/denied events, check scripts the implementer cannot read, deniedReadRoots enforced by the sandbox backends for shell/subprocess/terminal, and the judge role denied the same set.","workflows":["W2","W3"],"principles":["P4"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:trajectories","type":"package","title":"dsh-trajectories","summary":"Persisted sessions folded into dsh-trajectory/1 records with certificate-decided rewards (outcome 0 on the tamper basis whatever else the log holds), attempt counts, provenance, held-out withholding, and parity beside outcome as an auxiliary signal a training run may shape with but never optimize alone.","workflows":["W3","W4"],"principles":["P3","P6"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:components","type":"package","title":"dsh-components","summary":"Content-addressed component registry: componentDigest, componentAddress, and digestBasis mint a stable id@digest identity for every registered component, resolved through scoped global/agent layers that five per-seam adapters and the composition/manifest writer now read every step.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/components/components/README.md","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P1","P5"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:fs-read-barrier","type":"package","title":"dsh-fs-read-barrier","summary":"Enforces dsh-read-barrier's policy at the ctx.fs fs/read-intent gate, refusing a validator-owned read with FS_READ_BARRIER_DENIED before any read reaches the filesystem.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/fs/fs-read-barrier/README.md","workflows":["W2","W3"],"principles":["P4"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:shifts","type":"package","title":"dsh-shifts","summary":"Durable shift driver over the fleet: a content digest freezes each district's slot identity, shift/* events ledger it in the slot's own session log, resume runs exactly the cells that never started and records an orphan as interrupted, cadence and a spend window bound each district, and the fleet's fleet/cell event fires once per cell.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/improvement/shifts/README.md","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P3","P7"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:program","type":"package","title":"dsh-program","summary":"Durable program ledger for one client deliverable decomposed into many goals: a frozen ProgramSpec identified by its digest, one department session and git worktree per goal with its own budget/caps, an integration session whose certificate over the merged head releases, and resume that reconciles every goal from its own log and worktree without ever opening a second session for a key.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/improvement/program/README.md","workflows":["W2"],"principles":["P3","P7"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:judge","type":"package","title":"dsh-judge","summary":"The blind judge: a lineage-free session created with no parent or seed over a digest-checked copy of the audited workspace, whose derived history holds exactly the judge prompt, the task, and one evidence message naming verdicts never contents; judge/session and judge/verdict events record the audit, and the invariant rejects a verdict from a session that saw more.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/verification/judge/README.md","workflows":["W1","W2"],"principles":["P2","P4"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:components-tools","type":"package","title":"dsh-components-tools","summary":"Mirrors one scope's visible tool schemas into the component registry as tool components addressed by the model-facing schema alone, following tools/change so the inventory tracks exactly what the model can call.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/components/components-tools/README.md","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P1","P5"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:components-prompt","type":"package","title":"dsh-components-prompt","summary":"Mirrors one scope's resolved system-prompt sections into the component registry as prompt-section components, content-addressed for stored text and registration-addressed for a section a provider evaluates per assembly, following system-prompt/change.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/components/components-prompt/README.md","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P1","P5"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:components-presets","type":"package","title":"dsh-components-presets","summary":"Mirrors every standing agent-preset mount into the component registry as a preset component addressed by its resolved composition rows after include resolution, reconciled at agent/created and agent-preset/selected since the roster publishes no mount notification.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/components/components-presets/README.md","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P1","P5"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:components-manifest","type":"package","title":"dsh-components-manifest","summary":"Appends the composition/manifest session event: on every agent/pre-step it recomputes the agent's component inventory and records a new event only when the ordered id@digest addresses changed, so what produced a session's model requests is reconstructable from its log alone.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/components/components-manifest/README.md","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P3","P5"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:components-skills","type":"package","title":"dsh-components-skills","summary":"Mirrors every skill body a session has actually loaded, never one merely listed, into the component registry as a skill component addressed by the digest that load recorded, re-registering a body edited in place at its new address on skills/change.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/components/components-skills/README.md","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P1","P5"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"pkg:components-packages","type":"package","title":"dsh-components-packages","summary":"Mirrors mounted Loader entries as plugin components and live runtime-defined activations as synthesized dynamic-package components, so a session that mounted code it wrote at runtime is named in its own manifest.","url":"https://github.com/lbjlincoln/deepseek-harness/blob/claude/coding-agent-harness-u9l4gt/packages/components/components-packages/README.md","workflows":["W1","W4"],"principles":["P1","P5"],"source":"daliesk"},{"id":"2603.28052","type":"paper","title":"Meta-Harness: End-to-End Optimization of Model Harnesses","subtitle":"Yoonho Lee et al.","year":"2026-03","summary":"The performance of large language model (LLM) systems depends not only on model weights, but also on their harness: the code that determines what information to store, retrieve, and present to the model. Yet harnesses are still designed largely by hand, and existing text optimizers are poorly matched to this setting because they compress feedback too aggressively. We introduce Meta-Harness, an out","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.28052","themes":["harness-evolution"],"workflows":["W1","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2603.19461","type":"paper","title":"Hyperagents","subtitle":"Jenny Zhang et al.","year":"2026-03","summary":"Self-improving AI systems aim to reduce reliance on human engineering by learning to improve their own learning and problem-solving processes. Existing approaches to self-improvement rely on fixed, handcrafted meta-level mechanisms, fundamentally limiting how fast such systems can improve. The Darwin Gödel Machine (DGM) demonstrates open-ended self-improvement in coding by repeatedly generating an","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.19461","themes":["harness-evolution"],"workflows":["W1","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2606.26294","type":"paper","title":"The Red Queen Gödel Machine: Co-Evolving Agents and Their Evaluators","subtitle":"Alex Iacob et al.","year":"2026-06","summary":"Extends the Darwin/Huxley Gödel Machine lineage to non-stationary evaluation by co-evolving learned evaluators alongside the agents they score, using 'controlled utility evolution' (fixed-evaluator epochs with anchor-gated evaluator replacement) to preserve per-epoch self-improvement guarantees.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.26294","themes":["harness-evolution","long-running-agents"],"workflows":["W1","W4","W2"],"source":"literature","result":"On Polyglot coding, adding a co-evolved code reviewer exceeds prior SOTA (HGM) pass rate (71.7% vs. 69.9%) at 1.35-1.72x fewer search tokens; on paper writing/reviewing, co-evolved writers raise acceptance rate from HGM's 21.8% to 40.5%, and the co-evolved reviewer is corrected via an adversarial objective to stop over-accepting AI-generated text relative to human text (from 1.42-1.91x over-acceptance down to comparable rates).","principles":["P5","P6"]},{"id":"2607.03935","type":"paper","title":"Harness-Aware Self-Evolving: Co-Evolving Model Weights, Harness, and Task Solutions","subtitle":"Haochen Luo et al.","year":"2026-07","summary":"Self-evolving frameworks usually optimize task solutions while treating the surrounding harness as fixed. We introduce Harness-Aware Self-Evolving (HASE), an agentic reinforcement-learning framework in which a single model can generate task solutions or edit selected harness components in a multi-turn action space. HASE enables a single Qwen3-8B model to match the text-classification performance o","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.03935","themes":["harness-evolution"],"workflows":["W1","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.04968","type":"paper","title":"EvolveNet: Collaborative Harness Evolution for Agent Self-Improvement","subtitle":"Jun Nie et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"The capabilities of an LLM agent depend not only on its model but on the harness: the executable program that constructs context, invokes tools, verifies results, and recovers from failure. Recent work shows that evolving the harness yields persistent improvements without updating model weights. Existing approaches, however, assume that all execution experience can be routed to a single optimizer,","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04968","themes":["harness-evolution"],"workflows":["W1","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.07645","type":"paper","title":"Mendel Gödel Machine: Recursive Self-Improving Coding Agents via Comparative Evolution","subtitle":"Changzhi Liu et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"Self-improving coding agents that iteratively rewrite their own source code have demonstrated impressive performance on coding tasks. However, existing solutions generally derive self-modification from a single failure trajectory at a time, overlooking rich comparative signals available in the agent's expanding archive of past attempts. According to Mendelian principles of controlled inheritance, ","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.07645","themes":["harness-evolution"],"workflows":["W1","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2605.20086","type":"paper","title":"What Do Evolutionary Coding Agents Evolve?","subtitle":"Nico Pelleriti et al.","year":"2026-05","summary":"Recent work pairs LLMs with evolutionary search to iteratively generate, modify, and select code using task-specific feedback. These systems have produced strong results in mathematical discovery and algorithm design, yet a fundamental question remains: what do they actually evolve? Progress is typically summarized by the best score a run reaches under a task-specific evaluator, but that score can","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.20086","themes":["harness-evolution"],"workflows":["W1","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2507.19457","type":"paper","title":"GEPA: Reflective Prompt Evolution Can Outperform Reinforcement Learning","subtitle":"Lakshya A Agrawal et al.","year":"2025-07","summary":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly adapted to downstream tasks via reinforcement learning (RL) methods like Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), which often require thousands of rollouts to learn new tasks. We argue that the interpretable nature of language often provides a much richer learning medium for LLMs, compared to policy gradients derived from sparse, scalar rewards. To t","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19457","themes":["harness-evolution"],"workflows":["W1","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2510.06711","type":"paper","title":"Inefficiencies of Meta Agents for Agent Design","subtitle":"Batu El et al.","year":"2025-10","summary":"Recent works began to automate the design of agentic systems using meta-agents that propose and iteratively refine new agent architectures. In this paper, we examine three key challenges in a common class of meta-agents. First, we investigate how a meta-agent learns across iterations and find that simply expanding the context with all previous agents, as proposed by previous works, performs worse ","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06711","themes":["harness-evolution"],"workflows":["W1","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2503.13657","type":"paper","title":"Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail?","subtitle":"Mert Cemri et al.","year":"2025-03","summary":"Despite enthusiasm for Multi-Agent LLM Systems (MAS), their performance gains on popular benchmarks are often minimal. This gap highlights a critical need for a principled understanding of why MAS fail. Addressing this question requires systematic identification and analysis of failure patterns. We introduce MAST-Data, a comprehensive dataset of 1600+ annotated traces collected across 7 popular MA","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657","themes":["multi-agent-failure"],"workflows":["W2","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2512.08296","type":"paper","title":"Towards a Science of Scaling Agent Systems","subtitle":"Yubin Kim et al.","year":"2025-12","summary":"Agents, language model-based systems capable of reasoning, planning, and acting are widely adopted in real-world tasks, yet how their performance changes as these systems scale across key dimensions remains underexplored. We introduce quantitative scaling principles for agent systems as a predictive model, capturing how performance varies with coordination, model capability, and measurable system ","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.08296","themes":["multi-agent-failure"],"workflows":["W2","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.03836","type":"paper","title":"Resume Means Resume: A Machine-Checked Conformance Contract for Checkpoint, Interrupt, and Resume Semantics in Workflow Persistence Layers","subtitle":"Sajjad Khan et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"A framework that persists execution state so a run can be interrupted, survive a crash, and continue must decide what a resume means for effects that already happened. Five widely deployed agent workflow frameworks answer differently, none exposes a machine-checkable contract, and measured behavior violates even the fragments they state. The RESUME CONTRACT states six properties over the persisten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03836","themes":["multi-agent-failure"],"workflows":["W2","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.05263","type":"paper","title":"OrchestraBench: Evaluating Multi-Agent Orchestration Failure Modes, Recovery, and Decomposition Quality","subtitle":"Yidian Chen et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"Multi-agent orchestration frameworks are moving from demos to production, yet benchmarks typically report task accuracy without diagnosing why a pipeline failed, where a cascade began, or which routing decision caused the breakdown. OrchestraBench evaluates failure, recovery, and decomposition through a controlled, seed-reproducible failure-injection harness over templated enterprise workflows. It","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05263","themes":["multi-agent-failure"],"workflows":["W2","W4"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2605.22502","type":"paper","title":"Compiling Agentic Workflows into LLM Weights: Near-Frontier Quality at Two Orders of Magnitude Less Cost","subtitle":"Simon Dennis et al.","year":"2026-05","summary":"Agent orchestration frameworks have proliferated, collectively exceeding 290,000 GitHub stars across LangGraph, CrewAI, Google ADK, OpenAI Agents SDK, Semantic Kernel, Strands, and LlamaIndex. All follow the same pattern: an external orchestrator above the LLM, injecting instructions and routing decisions every turn. Recent work has shown this architecture is dominated for procedural tasks by simp","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22502","themes":["harness-native-rl"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.26557","type":"paper","title":"DeepRepro: State-Aware Subplanning for Paper-to-Code Reproduction in Evolving Repositories","subtitle":"Hongru Song et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"Recent advances in agentic large language models (LLMs) have enabled increasingly autonomous software engineering workflows, yet automatic machine learning (ML) paper-to-code reproduction remains a challenging long-horizon problem. Unlike conventional code generation, this task requires constructing and maintaining a fully functional repository whose state continuously evolves during execution. Ex","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26557","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.24291","type":"paper","title":"ReproAgent: Contract-Guided Paper-to-Code Reproduction","subtitle":"Xue Hu et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"Paper-to-code reproduction asks scientific AI agents to turn research papers into executable repositories that preserve the paper's method, protocol and artifacts. This is difficult because the specification is split: explicit paper content such as algorithms, metrics and artifacts is often lost across long agent trajectories, while implicit details such as framework defaults and conventions inher","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24291","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2609.02272","type":"paper","title":"PaperCompiler: Faithful Paper-to-Code Generation via Repository-Level Specification Compilation","subtitle":"Yunhao Liu et al.","year":"2026-09","summary":"Faithfully translating research papers into repository-level implementations remains challenging because papers often describe methods at a high level, leave implementation assumptions implicit, and require generated repositories to preserve method logic, evaluation protocols, and cross-file consistency. Despite recent advances in paper-to-code agents, their intermediate outputs are often presente","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02272","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.24252","type":"paper","title":"SA-Bench: Evaluating Semantic Alignment in LLM-Based Paper Reproduction","subtitle":"Xue Hu et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"LLM agents can generate paper reproduction code, yet often produce scientifically unfaithful implementations. We define this failure mode as semantic drift, where generated code silently diverges from the paper's specifications. We introduce SemanticAlign-Bench(SA-Bench), a diagnostic benchmark covering 30 papers from ICLR, ICML and NeurIPS 2025. For each paper, we decompose its specifications int","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24252","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2607.02931","type":"paper","title":"VERITAS: Towards a General-Purpose Replication Tool for Scientific Research","subtitle":"Haokun Liu et al.","year":"2026-07","summary":"AI tools are accelerating scientific publication while the systems that review it struggle to keep up, and independent verification of published research has become both harder and more important. As manual replication is slow and expensive, a growing line of work uses coding agents to automate parts of the process. Existing efforts are largely packaged as benchmarks with companion agents that onl","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.02931","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2504.01848","type":"paper","title":"PaperBench: Evaluating AI's Ability to Replicate AI Research","subtitle":"Giulio Starace et al.","year":"2025-04","summary":"We introduce PaperBench, a benchmark evaluating the ability of AI agents to replicate state-of-the-art AI research. Agents must replicate 20 ICML 2024 Spotlight and Oral papers from scratch, including understanding paper contributions, developing a codebase, and successfully executing experiments. For objective evaluation, we develop rubrics that hierarchically decompose each replication task into","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.01848","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2504.17192","type":"paper","title":"Paper2Code: Automating Code Generation from Scientific Papers in Machine Learning","subtitle":"Minju Seo et al.","year":"2025-04","summary":"Despite the rapid growth of machine learning research, corresponding code implementations are often unavailable, making it slow and labor-intensive for researchers to reproduce results and build upon prior work. In the meantime, recent Large Language Models (LLMs) excel at understanding scientific documents and generating high-quality code. Inspired by this, we introduce PaperCoder, a multi-agent ","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.17192","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2505.19955","type":"paper","title":"MLR-Bench: Evaluating AI Agents on Open-Ended Machine Learning Research","subtitle":"Hui Chen et al.","year":"2025-05","summary":"Recent advancements in AI agents have demonstrated their growing potential to drive and support scientific discovery. In this work, we introduce MLR-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating AI agents on open-ended machine learning research. MLR-Bench includes three key components: (1) 201 research tasks sourced from NeurIPS, ICLR, and ICML workshops covering diverse ML topics; (2) MLR-Judge","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.19955","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.04975","type":"paper","title":"SciCode-Verified: How Benchmark Defects Underestimated the Scientific-Coding Ability of Language Models","subtitle":"Sihan Hu et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"SciCode is the standard measure of the scientific-coding ability of language models: research-level problems that demand both frontier scientific theory and its implementation as working numerical code. It is a component of the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index and a standing evaluation in government and national-laboratory suites. Yet its scores have recently plateaued: the strongest 2026 mo","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04975","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2511.02824","type":"paper","title":"Kosmos: An AI Scientist for Autonomous Discovery","subtitle":"Ludovico Mitchener et al.","year":"2025-11","summary":"Data-driven scientific discovery requires iterative cycles of literature search, hypothesis generation, and data analysis. Substantial progress has been made towards AI agents that can automate scientific research, but all such agents remain limited in the number of actions they can take before losing coherence, thus limiting the depth of their findings. Here we present Kosmos, an AI scientist tha","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.02824","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2409.13740","type":"paper","title":"Language agents achieve superhuman synthesis of scientific knowledge","subtitle":"Michael D. Skarlinski et al.","year":"2024-09","summary":"Language models are known to hallucinate incorrect information, and it is unclear if they are sufficiently accurate and reliable for use in scientific research. We developed a rigorous human-AI comparison methodology to evaluate language model agents on real-world literature search tasks covering information retrieval, summarization, and contradiction detection tasks. We show that PaperQA2, a fron","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.13740","themes":["paper-to-code"],"workflows":["W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2606.07297","type":"paper","title":"SWE-Explore: Benchmarking How Coding Agents Explore Repositories","subtitle":"Shaoqiu Zhang et al.","year":"2026-06","summary":"Repository-level coding benchmarks such as SWE-bench have driven a rapid surge in the capabilities of coding agents. Yet they usually treat coding tasks as a holistic, binary prediction problem (e.g., resolved or unresolved), neglecting fine-grained agent capabilities such as repository understanding, context retrieval, code localization, and bug diagnosis. In this paper, we introduce SWE-Explore,","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.07297","themes":["repo-context"],"workflows":["W1","W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2602.05892","type":"paper","title":"ContextBench: A Benchmark for Context Retrieval in Coding Agents","subtitle":"Han Li et al.","year":"2026-02","summary":"LLM-based coding agents have shown strong performance on automated issue resolution benchmarks, yet existing evaluations largely focus on final task success, providing limited insight into how agents retrieve and use code context during problem solving. We introduce ContextBench, a process-oriented evaluation of context retrieval in coding agents. ContextBench consists of 1,136 issue-resolution ta","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05892","themes":["repo-context"],"workflows":["W1","W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2607.24882","type":"paper","title":"Agent Retrieval Bench: Evaluating Repository Context Retrieval for Coding Agents","subtitle":"Bowen Qin et al.","year":"2026-07","summary":"Modern coding agents are usually evaluated by whether they eventually produce a correct patch, but patch generation depends on an earlier context-acquisition stage: finding the repository files needed for the task. We introduce Agent Retrieval Bench, a file-level benchmark for this upstream retrieval problem. Samples are built from real coding-workflow signals and evaluated against frozen base-com","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.24882","themes":["repo-context"],"workflows":["W1","W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2606.11864","type":"paper","title":"CORE-Bench: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Code Retrieval in the Era of Agentic Coding","subtitle":"Fuwei Zhang et al.","year":"2026-06","summary":"Code retrieval is becoming central to coding agents, but agentic coding requires more than matching a natural-language query to an isolated snippet. Given a user request, a coding agent needs to navigate a concrete repository state, locate relevant files and functions, gather supporting context, and filter similar in-repository distractors. Existing code retrieval benchmarks mainly evaluate docstr","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11864","themes":["repo-context"],"workflows":["W1","W2"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.17528","type":"paper","title":"Agent Lightning v1.0: Towards Harnessed Agentic RL","subtitle":"Zhiyuan He et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"Modern agents operate inside agent harnesses that manage tools, context, and control flow, making the harness a critical part of the agent system. Our original Agent Lightning introduced a disaggregated architecture that connects arbitrary agents to RL training through an LLM endpoint proxy, an approach later adopted by frameworks such as verl Uni-Agent, AReaL 2.0, slime, and Polar. We refer to th","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17528","themes":["harness-native-rl"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.17393","type":"paper","title":"LEGO-RL: Harness-Native Reinforcement Learning for Coding Agents","subtitle":"Yiming Du et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"Reinforcement learning for coding agents increasingly relies on long-running agent harnesses to manage tool integration, repository contexts, and execution feedback. However, the native execution environments of these harnesses are inherently misaligned with policy-gradient training: environmental crashes and reward hacking corrupt outcome signals, while train-inference discrepancies decouple roll","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17393","themes":["harness-native-rl"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2605.24220","type":"paper","title":"Polar: Agentic RL on Any Harness at Scale","subtitle":"Binfeng Xu et al.","year":"2026-05","summary":"Reinforcement learning for language agents increasingly depends on custom harnesses that manage long-running context, multi-turn tool use and multi-agent orchestration. However, porting these harnesses into RL environment interfaces remains difficult and often loses important training signals. We bridge this gap with polar, a rollout framework for scalable asynchronous RL over arbitrary agent harn","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.24220","themes":["harness-native-rl"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2609.01245","type":"paper","title":"Explore More, Drift Less: Outcome-Only Reinforcement Learning Can Suffice for Long-Horizon Interactive Agents","subtitle":"Liming Pu et al.","year":"2026-09","summary":"Reinforcement learning is a natural way to post-train LLM agents for long-horizon interactive tasks judged only by end-of-task verification, yet a shared belief holds that outcome-only RL soon hits a ceiling on small open models. Recent work therefore compensates around the training with denser rewards, SFT priors, skill libraries, curated memory, or multi-agent orchestration. We argue the ceiling","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01245","themes":["harness-native-rl"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2608.14312","type":"paper","title":"Envs-FORGE: Frontier-Optimized Reward-Grounded Environment Synthesis for Agent RL","subtitle":"Xiaojun Wu et al.","year":"2026-08","summary":"Reinforcement learning (RL) for terminal agents needs executable training environments with reliable rewards and useful difficulty. Fixed recipes such as few-shot, Self-Instruct, and Evol-Instruct apply the same prompting policy to every seed, even when the current policy would benefit from a harder, easier, or simply different task. We present Envs-FORGE, a prompting policy that converts verifier","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14312","themes":["harness-native-rl"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2511.16108","type":"paper","title":"SkyRL-Agent: Efficient RL Training for Multi-turn LLM Agent","subtitle":"Shiyi Cao et al.","year":"2025-11","summary":"We introduce SkyRL-Agent, a framework for efficient, multi-turn, long-horizon agent training and evaluation. It provides efficient asynchronous dispatching, lightweight tool integration, and flexible backend interoperability, enabling seamless use with existing RL frameworks such as SkyRL-train, VeRL, and Tinker. Using SkyRL-Agent, we train SA-SWE-32B, a software engineering agent trained from Qwe","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16108","themes":["harness-native-rl"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2602.15763","type":"paper","title":"GLM-5: from Vibe Coding to Agentic Engineering","subtitle":" GLM-5-Team et al.","year":"2026-02","summary":"We present GLM-5, a next-generation foundation model designed to transition the paradigm of vibe coding to agentic engineering. Building upon the agentic, reasoning, and coding (ARC) capabilities of its predecessor, GLM-5 adopts DSA to significantly reduce training and inference costs while maintaining long-context fidelity. To advance model alignment and autonomy, we implement a new asynchronous ","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15763","themes":["open-models"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2512.20856","type":"paper","title":"NVIDIA Nemotron 3: Efficient and Open Intelligence","subtitle":" NVIDIA et al.","year":"2025-12","summary":"We introduce the Nemotron 3 family of models - Nano, Super, and Ultra. These models deliver strong agentic, reasoning, and conversational capabilities. The Nemotron 3 family uses a Mixture-of-Experts hybrid Mamba-Transformer architecture to provide best-in-class throughput and context lengths of up to 1M tokens. Super and Ultra models are trained with NVFP4 and incorporate LatentMoE, a novel appro","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.20856","themes":["open-models"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2504.21798","type":"paper","title":"SWE-smith: Scaling Data for Software Engineering Agents","subtitle":"John Yang et al.","year":"2025-04","summary":"Despite recent progress in Language Models (LMs) for software engineering, collecting training data remains a significant pain point. Existing datasets are small, with at most 1,000s of training instances from 11 or fewer GitHub repositories. The procedures to curate such datasets are often complex, necessitating hundreds of hours of human labor; companion execution environments also take up sever","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.21798","themes":["harness-native-rl"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2504.07164","type":"paper","title":"R2E-Gym: Procedural Environments and Hybrid Verifiers for Scaling Open-Weights SWE Agents","subtitle":"Naman Jain et al.","year":"2025-04","summary":"Improving open-source models on real-world SWE tasks (solving GITHUB issues) faces two key challenges: 1) scalable curation of execution environments to train these models, and, 2) optimal scaling of test-time compute. We introduce AgentGym, the largest procedurally-curated executable gym environment for training real-world SWE-agents, consisting of more than 8.7K tasks. AgentGym is powered by two","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07164","themes":["harness-native-rl"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"field-study"},{"id":"2508.18255","type":"paper","title":"Hermes 4 Technical Report","subtitle":"Ryan Teknium et al.","year":"2025-08","summary":"We present Hermes 4, a family of hybrid reasoning models that combine structured, multi-turn reasoning with broad instruction-following ability. We describe the challenges encountered during data curation, synthesis, training, and evaluation, and outline the solutions employed to address these challenges at scale. We comprehensively evaluate across mathematical reasoning, coding, knowledge, compre","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18255","themes":["open-models"],"workflows":["W3"],"source":"self-verifying-harness"},{"id":"2607.28147","type":"paper","titl

